{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>All the days of its closing-up.</b> Rashi explained: and not the days when he scraped off its plague. That is, the days immediately after the closing — I might exclude a definite one that scraped off its plague. That is, when it was designated for demolition, such as when it returned after removal and scraping and plastering and taking of the oath, and they did not demolish but only scraped — I might say that one who enters the house should not be impure. Scripture says <b>all the days</b> — to include that it is impure. This for you is Rashi, explained well. But according to the plain sense, the implication of the verse is that each and every verse should rest in its place in peace, and no one should depart from his fellow. How so? 'And if the plague returns' — whether after a short time or after a long time — after he removed the stones and the priest came and saw, and behold it spread etc. — that is, he returned and demolished the house immediately, and one who enters the house before removal and scraping and plastering — that is 'all the days of its closing-up, he is impure until evening,' and the one who lies down and eats. But if the priest does come after removal and scraping and plastering and sees, and behold it did not spread — that is, it did not return — he pronounces the house pure, and takes to purify the house etc." "<b>He is impure until evening.</b> After he has washed his flesh, as is the rule."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The compiler cites Rashi's midrashic reading, then rejects it in favor of a sequential plain-sense reading where each verse follows the narrative order of the house's inspection procedure."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:12:19.710604781Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}